Cybersecurity has evolved from a technical concern into a systemic governance and economic resilience issue. This transformation is particularly significant for large Greek organizations operating within critical infrastructure, financial services, energy, telecommunications, transportation, healthcare, and large public-sector bodies. The implementation of the NIS2 Directive and related European regulatory instruments has fundamentally altered the accountability landscape by elevating cybersecurity to boardlevel responsibility. This paper examines the structural exposure of large Greek organizations to cyber risk, analyzes the asymmetry between preventive investment and post-incident economic consequences, and proposes a governance-centered quantitative risk assessment model. Drawing on risk economics, enterprise risk management (ERM), operational resilience theory, and European regulatory frameworks, the study develops a strategic decision-making model incorporating expected loss calculation and Monte Carlo simulation logic.&nbsp;
<div>
 Cybersecurity is increasingly recognized not merely as a technical or operational challenge, but as a core governance and institutional resilience issue. As digital infrastructures expand across both public administration and critical economic sectors, cyber risk is becoming structurally embedded within organizational decision-making, regulatory exposure, and strategic capital allocation.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 While existing research often approaches cybersecurity from technological, compliance, or risk management perspectives, this paper advances a different analytical proposition: cyber risk must be understood as a governance-centered financial exposure that directly affects institutional stability, regulatory accountability, and board-level responsibility.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The study introduces a governance-oriented cyber risk capital model that integrates probabilistic loss estimation, regulatory amplification factors, and institutional resilience variables into a unified analytical framework. Rather than treating cybersecurity investment as a technical expenditure, the model conceptualizes it as a form of risk capital allocation necessary for preserving organizational continuity and compliance under conditions of systemic digital vulnerability.
</div>
<div>
 Using the Greek institutional and regulatory environment as an applied case, the paper explores how emerging European regulatory frameworks, particularly the NIS2 Directive, reshape the accountability landscape for public entities and private organizations operating within critical sectors. In this context, cyber incidents are not only operational disruptions but potential governance failures capable of triggering financial penalties, reputational damage, and long-term strategic instability.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The objective of the paper is therefore twofold. First, it proposes a structured methodology for quantifying cyber risk in governance terms, incorporating expected loss modelling and regulatory multipliers. Second, it advances a policy-relevant framework that links cybersecurity investment decisions to institutional resilience and compliance obligations, positioning cyber governance as a central element of modern organizational risk architecture.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The findings suggest that cybersecurity maturity in Greece remains largely compliancedriven rather than resilience-driven, thereby creating systemic vulnerabilities. The paper concludes with a governance transformation framework designed to enhance organizational and national-level resilience. &nbsp;
</div>

This paper develops a focus-based decision-theoretic framework for choice under non-probabilistic subjective uncertainty, embedded within a broader structure of heterogeneous uncertainty. Rather than evaluating actions through probability-weighted averages, the framework represents evaluation as being organized around focus states—salient contingencies that jointly reflect plausibility and consequence—thereby capturing decision environments in which evaluation concentrates on a small number of decisive scenarios instead of aggregating over the entire state space.&nbsp;
<div>
 The analysis distinguishes three domains of uncertainty: roulette risk, subjective state uncertainty—corresponding to horse-race uncertainty in the Anscombe–Aumann sense—and ignorance. These domains are evaluated through an ordered, domain-specific procedure rather than a single aggregate operator. Within this structure, anchoring and asymmetric evaluation across domains arise as structural consequences of preference consistency within a multi-stage Anscombe–Aumann framework, rather than as behavioral assumptions. In particular, cardinal possibility weights over the subjective (horse-race) domain are derived from preference comparisons under minimal axioms, and a τ -anchor governs cross-domain evaluation without assuming full reversibility across uncertainty domains.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Building on cardinal possibility-based representation results developed by Giang (2015, 2016), the framework clarifies how focus-based evaluation, anchoring, and partial reversibility emerge within a three-domain structure. Overall, the paper provides a parsimonious foundation for analyzing anchoring, asymmetry, and partial reversibility in decision making under heterogeneous uncertainty beyond purely probabilistic risk.&nbsp;
</div>
